{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"45ca412fae67a493","type":"group","x":640,"y":-280,"width":1640,"height":906,"color":"3","label":"Gates with leakage results"},
		{"id":"878059622f7705a3","type":"group","x":660,"y":-20,"width":540,"height":587,"label":"Single trajectories"},
		{"id":"d59dd7da11ec7ac9","type":"group","x":1300,"y":-20,"width":440,"height":626,"label":"U = 150MHz"},
		{"id":"80dd73dc726715ca","type":"group","x":1820,"y":-16,"width":440,"height":622,"label":"U = 250MHz"},
		{"id":"e89e21dae1dbf8b9","type":"file","file":"Data/Driving/SingleQubitDriveLRURWA/traj/U150/without_diss.png","x":1320,"y":0,"width":400,"height":267},
		{"id":"925b1c842d423bab","type":"file","file":"Data/Driving/SingleQubitDriveLRURWA/traj/U150/with_diss.png","x":1320,"y":319,"width":400,"height":267},
		{"id":"66f5591d8cee6824","type":"file","file":"Data/Driving/SingleQubitDriveLRURWA/traj/without_diss.png","x":1840,"y":4,"width":400,"height":267},
		{"id":"2100724deca5a755","type":"file","file":"Data/Driving/SingleQubitDriveLRURWA/traj/with_diss.png","x":1840,"y":319,"width":400,"height":267},
		{"id":"89102f364fa9d9b1","type":"file","file":"Data/Driving/SingleQubitDriveLRURWA/good.png","x":671,"y":0,"width":260,"height":173},
		{"id":"2ad69f3519996c2f","type":"text","text":"Here the drive frequency is $1.002\\omega_1$.","x":960,"y":0,"width":220,"height":87},
		{"id":"8ec9e538afab101a","type":"file","file":"Data/Driving/SingleQubitDriveLRURWA/decent.png","x":671,"y":187,"width":260,"height":173},
		{"id":"b3d792bd732b81fa","type":"file","file":"Data/Driving/SingleQubitDriveLRURWA/bad.png","x":671,"y":373,"width":260,"height":173},
		{"id":"dea09dd973993f79","type":"text","text":"The results vary drastically! What transmons to use as a LRU should be carefully chosen.","x":960,"y":241,"width":220,"height":179},
		{"id":"46f2cf072e452dbe","type":"text","text":"Gate used is the x-gate (a + a_dag), with gaussian envelope with cutoff. In general the drive is same as the first transmon transition frequency. RWA was used to get more stabile results for a smaller time-step.","x":739,"y":-260,"width":385,"height":156},
		{"id":"18aaeba5afa3d3dc","x":1320,"y":-243,"width":350,"height":122,"type":"text","text":"The gates generate leakage while the LRU removes it, removing leakage faster the more there is of it. This results in a steady state."},
		{"id":"c07eb2a7a2c78ba3","x":2580,"y":53,"width":250,"height":240,"color":"4","type":"text","text":"While our LRU is not capable of removing all the leakage, it does bound the total leakage, so that leakage cannot fully occupy the circuit. Also, the LRU works for small amounts of leakage."},
		{"id":"cf272fb1a132cda5","x":2580,"y":480,"width":255,"height":123,"color":"1","type":"text","text":"Are the parameters not realistic? Or chosen for our benefit? Especially the gate repetition time $1\\mu s$?"},
		{"id":"5c86774d7df7ac41","type":"text","text":"Assuming that when leakage happens our state is exactly |2> is not realistic, it is better to realize gates and have them generate leakage.","x":-280,"y":68,"width":264,"height":184},
		{"id":"ebad29d1dce194f6","type":"text","text":"How does leakage look when we generate leakage via gates?","x":193,"y":115,"width":251,"height":117,"color":"1"}
	],
	"edges":[
		{"id":"960a96098bc785f8","fromNode":"5c86774d7df7ac41","fromSide":"right","toNode":"ebad29d1dce194f6","toSide":"left"},
